## What Is Collection Framework?
Collection Framework is a library of ready-made data structures provided by Java.
## Why we need it ?
Because for specific requirements if i want list structure or stack structure why should i make
every data structure from scratch instead java provides collection frameworks which has all data structures you want to use.
------------------------------
## What is List?
A List is simple ordered items collection in a way it is inserted and duplicated are allowed in list.
## What Is ArrayList?
A List implemented using an internal array. main thing is array list is not fixed size it resize automatically.
## What is LinkedList?
A linked list is a linear data structure where elements are not stored in side-by-side memory locations.
Instead, it forms a chain of separate items called nodes, where each node points to the next one using a reference or pointer.

[ Data | Next ] ---> [ Data | Next ] ---> [ Data | NULL ]

------------------------------
## What is Set?
A Set is a collection that cannot contain duplicate items.
If you try to add an item that is already there, it will just ignore it.
## What Is HashSet?
A Set that uses a hash table internally. Main thing is it does not keep any order of items.
Elements can come out in a totally random order, but it is extremely fast for searching items.
## What Is TreeSet?
A Set that uses a tree structure internally.
Main thing is it automatically stores its items in a sorted, ascending order.
------------------------------
## What is Queue?
A Queue is a collection used to hold items before processing them.
It follows the FIFO (First-In-First-Out) rule, meaning the first item you put in is the first item that comes out.
## What Is PriorityQueue?
A Queue where items are processed based on their priority, not just arrival time.
By default, the smallest item always stays at the top of the queue and comes out first.
------------------------------
## What is Map?
A Map is a completely separate structure that stores data in Key and Value pairs.
You cannot have duplicate keys, and every key maps to exactly one value.
## What Is HashMap?
A Map that uses a hash table internally. Main thing is it does not maintain any order of your keys,
but it lets you find any value instantly if you know its key.
## What Is TreeMap?
A Map that uses a tree structure internally. Main thing is it automatically sorts all your keys in an ascending order.
------------------------------
## Big-O Cheat Sheet for Performance

| Data Structure | Search / Access Time | Insert / Delete Time | Main Use Case |

| ArrayList | O(1) Fast | O(N) Slow | When you need to read data by index frequently. |
| LinkedList | O(N) Slow | O(1) Fast | When you are constantly adding or removing items from ends. |
| HashSet | O(1) Fast | O(1) Fast | When you want absolute unique items and fast lookups. |
| TreeSet | O(log N) Medium | O(log N) Medium | When you want unique items but they must stay sorted. |
| PriorityQueue | O(1) for Top element | O(log N) Medium | When you always need the minimum or maximum item next. |
| HashMap | O(1) Fast | O(1) Fast | When you need to look up values instantly using a unique key. |
| TreeMap | O(log N) Medium | O(log N) Medium | When you need key-value pairs but keys must stay sorted. |


